  h1="How We Verify"
  intro="Verification here means something specific and checkable, not a badge we hand out for free. Here's exactly what we check, how, and how often — including where we come up short."
>
  <h2>Source tiers for everything we publish</h2>
  <p>
    Every factual claim on this site — in an article or a directory listing — is weighed
    against where it came from. We treat these as a hierarchy, not interchangeable:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Government / primary regulator</strong> — the CFPB, FTC, Florida Attorney
      General, Florida courts and statutes, Miami-Dade County government.</li>
    <li><strong>Established nonprofits</strong> — the NFCC, legal aid organizations,
      recognized counseling bodies.</li>
    <li><strong>Primary organization sources</strong> — a provider's own official page or
      published documentation.</li>
    <li><strong>Secondary reporting</strong> — local newspapers and reputable publications.</li>
    <li><strong>Community observations</strong> — forums and social discussion. We use these
      only to notice what people are asking about, never as a cited fact.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What gets checked on a directory listing</h2>
  <p>Before an organization appears in the directory, and on every re-check afterward, we look at:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Whether it's currently operating and reachable at the listed contact information.</li>
    <li>Regulatory or licensing status where one actually applies — Florida OFR registration
      for collection agencies, Florida Bar standing for attorneys, NCUA status for credit
      unions, HUD approval for housing counselors — checked against the official public source
      for each, not the organization's own claim alone.</li>
    <li>Published fee structure, eligibility criteria, languages offered, and service area, using
      the organization's own official page wherever possible.</li>
    <li>Public complaint and enforcement records where an accessible public index exists (CFPB,
      BBB) — recorded as what that specific index shows, not treated as a final verdict either
      way.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every listing shows a last-verified date and gets re-checked on a set schedule rather than
    published once and forgotten.
  </p>

  <h2>What "Unverified / Requires Direct Call" means</h2>
  <p>
    Some things — a specific fee, an income cutoff, whether a language is actually offered by
    phone versus only in person — aren't published anywhere official, and no amount of searching
    will surface a fact that isn't public. When that happens, we label the field exactly that way
    instead of guessing, estimating, or leaving the impression that it was confirmed. An absence
    of a public record is also not treated as proof of a clean record — a provider with no public
    complaints on file gets recorded as exactly that, not as "verified clean."
  </p>

  <h2>What gets checked on an article</h2>
  <p>
    Every guide cites its sources by name, states when it was last reviewed, and — for the
    highest-stakes topics — carries a "what's changed since our last review" section instead of
    a silently bumped date. We never publish a Miami-Dade-specific statistic that isn't publicly
    available; if only a national figure exists, we say so and label it national rather than
    implying a local number we don't actually have.
  </p>

  <h2>When we get it wrong</h2>
  <p>
    We will. Regulations change, organizations close, phone numbers stop working. If you find
    something outdated or incorrect, <a href="/report-an-error/">report it</a> — see also
    <a href="/editorial-independence/">Editorial Independence</a> for how that's handled when the
    listing in question is a paid one.
  </p>
</PolicyLayout>
